      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fairmead was founded in 1912 as Colony 13, a planned agricultural settlement by Cooperative Land and Title Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fairmead_california",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Mennonite farmers from Germany and Russia settled Fairmead, drawn by fertile soil and irrigation, shaping its early crop focus on alfalfa and dairy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fairmead_california",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A creamery, cheese factory, lumber yard, and implement dealers sprang up quickly, supporting dairy production and general farming in the early years.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fairmead_california",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The community’s soils are fine‑loamy Madera series with high clay, causing shrink‑swell and a water table that rises 3–6 feet in winter, leading to seasonal ponding.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/fairmead_california",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Fairmead’s post office operated from 1913 to 1940, marking the town’s early communication link before the railroad era.",
